+            <t indent="0" pn="section-3.4-22.2.2">These original packets are destined to another node; they are transmitted on a link towards their destination.</t>
+          </dd>
+          <dt pn="section-3.4-22.3">(l.o.3) a tunnel upper-layer</dt>
+          <dd pn="section-3.4-22.4">
+            <t indent="0" pn="section-3.4-22.4.1">(path #7, Fig.5)</t>
+            <t indent="0" pn="section-3.4-22.4.2">These packets undergo another decapsulation; they were nested tunnel packets.  This node is both the exit- point node of an outer tunnel and one or more inner tunnels.</t>
+          </dd>
+        </dl>
+        <t indent="0" pn="section-3.4-23">Implementation Note:</t>
+        <t indent="0" pn="section-3.4-24">The tunnel link-layer input and output can be implemented similar to the input and output of other link-layer protocols, for instance, associating an interface or pseudo-interface with the IPv6 tunnel.</t>
+        <t indent="0" pn="section-3.4-25">The selection of the &quot;IPv6 tunnel link&quot; over other links results from the packet forwarding decision taken based on the content of the node's routing table.</t>
